    <string name="help_section">The section is the part of the newspaper your article belongs to – for example sport, animals or school. It helps everyone find your article.</string>
    <string name="help_kicker">The kicker stands in small letters above the headline. In a few words it says where or what it is about – like a sign above a door.</string>
    <string name="help_headline">The headline is the big title. It should make people curious so that everyone wants to read your article.</string>
    <string name="help_subheadline">The subheadline stands right below the headline and tells a little more.</string>
    <string name="help_lead_image">The lead image is the big photo for your article. It shows at a glance what it is about.</string>
    <string name="help_caption">The caption stands below the photo. It says what or who you can see in it.</string>
    <string name="help_lead">The lead is the short first paragraph. In two or three sentences it tells the most important things: who, what, when, where.</string>
    <string name="help_paragraph">A paragraph is a piece of your text. When a new thought begins, you start a new paragraph.</string>
    <string name="help_subhead">A subhead is a small title in the middle of the text. It divides a long article into parts.</string>
    <string name="help_quote">A quote is the exact words someone said. Write who said them.</string>
    <string name="help_list">A bullet list puts several things one below the other, each with a dot in front – like a shopping list.</string>
    <string name="help_image">An image in the text is a photo in the middle of your article, right where it fits. Write below it what you can see.</string>
    <string name="help_image_rights">Important with photos: ask every person who can be recognised in the photo whether it may go into the newspaper – for children, ask their parents too. If someone says no or you cannot ask, choose another photo or have the faces made unrecognisable (pixelated) – an adult will help you with that. Only use photos you took yourself or are allowed to use.</string>
